| Synopsis1               = In a flashback to her first death,  [[Jennifer Walters (Earth-616)|Jen Walters]] remembers being being shot in the back, her cousin [[Bruce Banner (Earth-616)|Bruce Banner]]'s voice assuring her that she was going to be all right, and then falling down a vertical shaft towards a [[Green Door]]. Waking up in the [[Below-Place]] in the Hellish ruins of a city, Jen finds herself looking up at [[One Below All (Multiverse)|a monstrous face in the sky]], which declares that she bears its mark. At the time, Jen had dismissed it as a strange vision brought on by her near-death experience and passing back through the [[Green Door]] had erased her memories about it. Upon awakening in a hospital bed, she had learned that the Mob had tried to assassinate her, triggering her first transformation into the [[She-Hulk]].

In the present day, in the wake of the [[Empyre (Event)|Cotati invasion]], Jen - now simply going by the name of [[Hulk]] and sporting a bulky form - punches [[Tantrum (Mutant) (Earth-616)|Tantrum]] in the face; mentally noting that the mutant grows more powerful by feeding off fear and that the chaos of the war is like a buffet to him. Wiping blood away from his broken nose, Tantrum boasts that he is just leeching off her rage to grow more powerful, but Hulk cuts him off by punching him in the face again. Before Tantrum can counterattack, [[James Howlett (Earth-616)|Wolverine]] stabs him in the back with his claws, killing him. Apologizing for interrupting her fight, Logan says it did not look like the brute force approach was working. Hulk replies that she did not mind the help, but asks why he killed Tantrum. Smirking, Wolverine retracts his claws and says they have [[Five (Earth-616)|special healers]] on [[Krakoa (Earth-616)|Krakoa]] that can bring dead mutants back to life. Logan notes that, while Tantrum broke mutant law and will face judgement for his crimes, he technically still has a place on Krakoa like any other mutant. Hulk scoffs that it must be nice for those who get to live there, Wolverine retorting that mutants have been feared and hated for centuries and deserve a place where they can be safe; and that Jen - who has been loved and adored as a member of the Avengers - has no right to comment on that. Considering his words, Hulk asks if she can speak to him somewhere quiet; transforming back to her human form and saying she needs proper sentences and a beer.

Sitting at a table in a pub, Jen remarks that it is quiet. Getting some beer bottles from behind the bar, Logan says that it took a while for the joint to get on its feet and that the re-opening was supposed to happen the next day. As Logan says that he is friends with the owner, who gave him a key, Jen quips that she saw him pick the lock with his claws; Logan retorting that he never said he had the key on him. Popping the caps with his claws, Logan asks what she wanted to talk about, remarking that he is sick of hearing "The Hulk has it worse" speeches from her cousin. He is taken aback when Jen instead asks him what dying and coming back to life was like. Scowling, Logan says that that is a more complicated question to answer than she has any way of comprehending, and a topic that it is not a good idea to talk about. As Jen presses him for further information, Logan retorts that he does not want to tell her and she does not want to know. Jen tells Logan that the Cotati had killed her, but now she is obviously alive and well; adding that this is the third or fourth time that she has died and come back. Jen states that she knows Logan has died numerous times - having been [[Death of Wolverine|entombed in adamantium]] on one occasion and reduced to his adamantium skeleton by nukes multiple times - and says that, while most attribute his survival to his healing factor, she wants to know if he can offer her any advice on how to cope. Pausing, Logan asks if she is still herself. Jen replies that she is, so Logan tells her to just attribute it to her own healing factor and stop overthinking things; warning her that looking too deeply into it risks making it impossible to do the work that needs doing.

Jen recalls that, a couple of years ago, a precognitive mutant named [[Ulysses Cain (Earth-616)|Ulysses]] had foreseen [[Thanos (Earth-616)|Thanos the Mad Titan]] destroying the world with a [[Cosmic Cube]]. Left with only three hours to stop him, the Avengers had taken him on at the cost of Colonel [[James Rhodes (Earth-616)|James Rhodes]]' life, and that the She-Hulk herself had taken an anti-Thanos missile to the chest, leaving her mortally wounded. She remembers waking up in a hospital bed to [[Anthony Stark (Earth-616)|Iron Man]] vehemently arguing with [[Carol Danvers (Earth-616)|Captain Marvel]] over the ethics of using Ulysses' powers to prevent crimes. Enraged by him essentially saying that they should not have gotten the warning that had saved the planet - albeit at the cost of the War Machine's life - Jen notes that, had she been in any condition to act, she would have taken Carol's side and broken Tony's jaw for letting his unbearable ego put innocent lives in danger and invalidate his own best friend's sacrifice. Jen remembers telling Carol to [[Civil War II|fight for her beliefs]] before her heart gave out from the anger she felt towards Tony... and that she does not remember what happened next.

Finding herself in the Below-Place for the second time, Jen found herself confronted by [[Brian Banner (Earth-616)|Brian Banner]], and remembered that, when she was a child, her parents had told her that he had murdered his own wife. Asking Brian if she is in [[Hell]], Jen was surprised when he said that the realm that they are in is ''much'' worse. Brian recalled that he had gone to Hell after his death, but managed to escape and return to the living world, seeking to purge the Hulk from his son. Jen rebuked him, saying that he had earned his place in Hell and that his return involved transforming into a gigantic reptilian monster that she had helped slay. Frowning, Brian said that he indeed recalls his second death, and that after it he had fallen past Hell into their present location - the [[Below-Place]]. Quipping that Hell and [[Hell Lords (Earth-616)|its rulers]] seem quaint to him now, Brian jubilantly stated that the [[Below-Place]] underpins the [[Multiverse]] and is the bedrock of Hell; and that they and all other [[Glossary:Gamma Mutates|Gamma Mutates]] bear its mark and contagion. Jen sarcastically asked if she is stuck here with him forever just because she got the wrong dose of radiation; Brian replying that she is not. As the [[Green Door]] started to open behind her, Brian replied that [[Leonard Samson (Earth-616)|Leonard Samson]] had been there just before her, and that he had left through a [[Green Door]] and returned to life. Lamenting that the [[Green Door]] does not open for him yet, Brian's pupils ominously glow green as he said that he can look through them and is learning much. Pulled through her [[Green Door]], Jen was returned to life and her memories of the encounter were erased.

In the present day, Hulk works out at the Avengers' gym; brooding over the trauma of multiple deaths still haunts her, and that while as a Hulk she should always be in control, she had felt broken and tainted - causing her transformation into the Gray She-Hulk. Spotting [[Thor Odinson (Earth-616)|Thor]] walking past, Hulk asks if he wants to train with her. As they exercise, Hulk remarks that she likes [[Power Cosmic|his new costume]] but misses the beard; Thor stating that he had liked it too, but that after he became the All-Father it was like looking at [[Odin Borson (Earth-616)|his own father]] in the mirror. As Thor laments that being the king is not how he had always imagined it, Hulk comments that he has eternity to get used to it. Thor somberly remarks that it must seem like eternity to mortals such as herself, but that he is ashamed of how little changing most gods seem to do. Hulk interrupts and tells him that she is not mortal, leaving him confused. Hulk explains that whenever she dies she comes back to life, effectively rendering her immortal; then asks what immortality is like from the other end. Thor asks if she would not rather discuss this as Jen, but Hulk replies that Jen is tired and that the subject matter is too heavy for her to withstand. Thor sadly tells her that it will only get heavier, saying that she cannot yet see the end point; adding that he knows not whether his own fated end is written [[Earth-14412|millennia hence]] or sooner than he would like, it is written just the same. Recalling [[Galan (Earth-616)|Galactus]], Thor states that Galan had survived the end of his own iteration of the [[Multiverse]], the birth of another, and billions of years after that; only to meet his end by Thor's own hand. Hulk is shocked that Thor killed Galactus, Thor saying that, at the time, it had felt like justice, but that, regardless, the deed is done. Thor morosely states that Galactus' death is proof that there is no such thing as immortality, and that eventually everything will end - even himself and her. 

Jen recalls her third death at the hands of the Cotati - berating Tony Stark for having been arrogant and stupid enough to trust them. Jen admits that she had not seen the Cotati's betrayal coming either, and that while people assume that she is as stupid as the Hulk, she is ''not'' - she just finds it hard to put her thoughts into words - she had been a fool then and paid the ultimate price for it. Ensnared by Centaurian Ironwood, she had been hollowed out and her corpse used as a puppet so that the Cotati could infiltrate the Avengers - or so she had been told.

In the [[Below-Place]], Jen was cheerfully greeted by the [[Samuel Sterns (Earth-616)|Leader]], asking if this is some pocket dimension that he has created and if he is in league with the Cotati. As her memories of her previous trips to the [[Below-Place]] start returning, she recalls that the [[Green Door]] will bring her back to life as it has Bruce, Leonard, and even Samuel himself. Gleeful, the Leader states that was how he discovered the [[Green Door]] in the first place, leading him to the [[One Below All (Multiverse)|One Below All]]. As the Leader boasts that his new plans span to the end of time, which he will witness as a [[Glossary:Gamma Mutates|Gamma Mutate]], Jen reminds him that the other Gamma Mutates - herself included - do not like him very much. As she quips that she and the other heroic Gamma Mutates will be using his oversized head as a football until the end of time, the Leader grins wickedly and tells her that she is wrong. 

Waking up from a night terror, Jen transforms into Hulk - recalling that, after she came back, she had not had time to question it until after the fighting ended. Recalling the advice that Wolverine and Thor had given her, Jen muses that they might be right because something happened the last time that she died that has left her terrified of dying again.

In the [[Below-Place]], the Leader smugly informs Jen that she will not even remember having this conversation, and that she and her allies are not immortal either. Conjuring a [[Green Door]] - the size of a playing card - in his hand, the Leader says that they just have the key to it, but that locks can always be changed. Rotating the [[Green Door]] to reveal a [[Red Door]], the Leader says that he now controls whether the [[Green Door]] opens or closes. Grinning wickedly, he says that he will let Jen resurrect since she has caught him in a good mood, but warning her that if she ever dies again, he will ''not'' be so magnanimous. 

In the present day, Hulk huddles on her bed, thinking to herself that she must not ''ever'' die again - with the Leader's shadow looming over her.

